  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/73cbae2c39fb2466fd59ced196c08ff2b6131b08a6a5dd4933e736404b12266e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
